\import{macros}
\import{lean-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\title{A complete layer-first method}

\p{The three-layer model in [the complete cube account](frap-0016)
turns a human solve into a sequence of stabilizer problems. We keep the
fixed frame and write #{U,D,F,B,R,L} for clockwise quarter-turns as seen
from the named face. A prime is an inverse and a superscript #{2} is a
half-turn; a word is read from left to right. The bottom face #{D} is the
first layer, so its center fixes the target colour and the four side centres
fix the cross alignment. The notation and face-view convention agree with
the [cube notation notes](uts-016J).}

\subtree{
  \title{Effects, setup, and preservation}

  \p{A \vocab{slot} is a destination for one corner or edge. A setup word
  #{s} brings a chosen case to the working slot; a trigger #{t} performs the
  local change there; and #{s t s^{-1}} transports that change to another
  location. This is conjugation in the move group, not a promise that the
  transported word has the same visible support before the replay is
  checked. A commutator}
  ##{ [a,b]=a b a^{-1}b^{-1} }
  \p{is useful when #{a} and #{b} overlap in a controlled way. Its support
  must still be computed in the current convention: cancellation in the
  group word does not by itself prove that every other piece is fixed.}

  \p{Let #{H_i} be the subgroup fixing everything completed before stage
  #{i}. A stage is valid when each of its words lies in #{H_i} after the
  setup data have been restored. Thus a solve follows a descending chain}
  ##{G\supseteq H_{\rm cross}\supseteq H_{\rm first}\supseteq
      H_{\rm middle}\supseteq H_{\rm last\,orientation}.}
  \p{The inclusions describe preservation, while the case table below gives
  the progress that makes the chain terminate. This separates the algebraic
  question “what does the word change?” from the practical questions of
  recognition, memory, and execution.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{The complete seven-stage method}

  \p{The following method is complete under the stated case conventions.
  At every stage, rotate only the working view or use a setup word; never
  rotate the whole cube while a positional convention is being recorded. A
  displayed algorithm is a witness for its local effect. Its inverse and a
  conjugate supply the other orientations and slots.}

  \ol{
    \li{\strong{Cross.} Solve the four #{D}-edges one at a time, matching
    each side colour to its centre. If a target edge is in the top layer,
    bring it above its slot and insert it with #{F^2} or a quarter-turn
    setup. If it is in the first layer with the wrong orientation, eject it
    to the top and insert it again. Earlier cross edges are kept out of the
    working slot.}

    \li{\strong{First-layer corners.} Put a target corner above its #{D}
    slot at #{UFR}. Repeat}
    ##{T=R' D' R D}
    \li{until the target sticker faces down and the two side stickers match.
    The word #{T} fixes the already solved cross after the setup is restored;
    at most four repetitions are needed. A corner incorrectly occupying the
    slot is first ejected with the same trigger, then treated as a top-layer
    case.}

    \li{\strong{Middle edges.} With the solved layer on the bottom, align a
    top-layer edge with its side centre. To insert it on the right use}
    ##{I_R=U R U' R' U' F' U F,}
    \p{and to insert it on the left use}
    ##{I_L=U' L' U L U F U' F'.}
    \p{Both words restore the first layer. If a middle edge is misplaced,
    use either insertion to eject it, then solve the resulting top-layer
    case.}

    \li{\strong{Last-layer cross.} Ignore the side colours and orient the
    four #{U}-edges with}
    ##{X=F R U R' U' F'.}
    \p{With the #{U}-face viewed from above, a line is held horizontal and
    an L is held at the back-left. The same word changes the zero-, two-,
    or four-oriented-edge cases to a case with strictly more oriented
    edges after the indicated setup; repeat until the cross is present.}

    \li{\strong{Last-layer edges.} First restore the side colours of the
    four cross edges. The checked three-edge cycle}
    ##{P=R U' R U R U R U' R' U' R^2}
    \p{fixes the corners and cycles three #{U}-edges. Together with a
    #{U}-turn, its conjugates generate all permutations of the four
    cross edges: a 4-cycle and a 3-cycle generate #{S_4}. Apply a setup,
    one or more copies of #{P} or #{P^{-1}}, and undo the setup.}

    \li{\strong{Last-layer corners.} Put a correctly located corner at
    #{UFR}; if none is located, apply the word once and inspect again. The
    corner cycle}
    ##{C=R' F R' B^2 R F' R' B^2 R^2}
    \p{fixes the cross edges and cycles the three other #{U}-corners. Its
    conjugates and #{U}-turns generate #{S_4}, so repeated applications
    place all four corners without changing their orientations.}

    \li{\strong{Last-layer corner orientation.} Keep the cube fixed. At
    #{UFR}, repeat #{T=R'D'RD} until that corner is oriented; turn only
    #{U} to bring the next unsolved corner to #{UFR}, and repeat. During
    this stage the visible lower layers look disturbed, but after the last
    corner the accumulated orientation sum is zero and every earlier piece
    returns to its solved position.}
  }

  \p{The method uses only a small vocabulary of triggers. The setup words
  are the memory burden; each trigger has a recognizable local effect. The
  two places where an algorithm appears to disrupt completed material (the
  corner orientation stage and an ejection of a misplaced piece) are
  deliberate: the word is applied until the current local case is solved,
  after which the preservation invariant is restored.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Why the cases terminate}

  \p{We now justify completeness rather than infer it from successful
  demonstrations. A cross edge can always be moved to the top layer or
  ejected from its slot, and a quarter-turn or half-turn inserts it into a
  matching slot. Removing one solved edge leaves a connected set of
  unsolved slots, so four repetitions finish the cross. The corner trigger
  has a nonzero twist effect at #{UFR} and fixes the cross after each full
  insertion; the eject-and-reinsert rule therefore solves four corners. The
  two middle insertions are inverses after reflection of the working view,
  and the same ejection argument solves four middle edges.}

  \p{For the last layer, the edge-orientation word #{X} changes the
  orientation pattern in the standard line/L/point cases while fixing both
  completed layers. The finite list of patterns has representatives point,
  line, and L; the prescribed setup makes the number of oriented edges
  increase, so the process reaches the cross. After orientation, #{P} is a
  3-cycle on edges and #{U} is a 4-cycle. These generate #{S_4}, proving
  that the edge-position stage reaches every permutation. The analogous
  checked effect of #{C}, together with #{U}, gives #{S_4} on corners.}

  \p{Finally, #{T} changes the orientation of the working corner while the
  temporary permutation cancels after the corner is finished and the next
  corner is brought into place. The corner orientation sum from
  [the cube constraints](frap-0016) is zero, so the final corner is forced
  when the other three have been oriented. Thus every state satisfying the
  three reachability tests has a finite word supplied by these stages. This
  is a completeness proof for the method, not an optimality claim.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{One annotated local solve}

  \p{Suppose the first two layers are solved, the #{U}-cross is oriented,
  and the three-edge cycle needed to place the #{U}-edge at the back is
  visible. Hold that edge at the slot to be fixed and apply #{P}; if the
  cycle runs the other way, apply #{P^{-1}}. The corners and all lower
  pieces return to their previous states. If the desired edge is not in
  the working support, choose a setup #{s} that brings it there and replay}
  ##{s P s^{-1}.}
  \p{After the edge positions are correct, use the same inspect--setup--cycle
  pattern for #{C}. Only then use #{T} at #{UFR}, counting quarter-turns
  until the corner's #{U}-sticker points up. The visible disorder during
  this last replay is expected; the final inverse setup and the orientation
  sum certify that the solved state has returned. This is the smallest
  complete trace of the method: recognize a case, transport a checked
  effect, and finish inside the next stabilizer.}
}

\p{A block-building or Roux-style organization can reduce the number of
stages for an experienced solver, but it changes the recognition and
preservation cases. It should be compared through the same questions—what
is fixed, what subgroup remains, which effects are checked, and what is
remembered—rather than by treating a lower move count as a complete measure
of usability. The [cube resource notes](uts-016J) collect contrasting
organizations for that later comparison.}
